The task is to define the primary symbolic meaning of "Ship" in Christian teaching and biblical interpretation, remaining blind to any author's position.
{
  "primary_meaning": "The Church as a vessel or ship navigating through the world, with Christ as the pilot or captain, symbolizing salvation and the journey of faith.

content.text:
{
  "primary_meaning": "The Church as the vessel of salvation navigating the waters of the world, storms of trial, or judgment (often linked to Noah's ark as type)",
  "primary_basis": [
    "Genesis 6–9 (Noah's ark as saving vessel)",
    "1 Peter 3:20–21 (ark prefiguring baptism and salvation through Christ)",
    "Matthew 8:23–27 / Mark 4:35–41 / Luke 8:22–25 (Jesus calming the storm in the boat with the disciples)",
    "Matthew 14:22–33 (Jesus walking on water to the boat)"
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "meaning": "The individual Christian life or soul journeying through the sea of worldly troubles toward heaven",
      "basis": [
        "Same Gospel storm and boat narratives applied devotionally",
        "Patristic and later spiritual writings on the soul as ship"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Worldly commerce, wealth, or the transient fortunes of this age",
      "basis": [
        "Revelation 18:17–19 (ships and merchants mourning Babylon)",
        "Ezekiel 27 (ships of Tyre as commercial power)"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "A temporary refuge or instrument of divine providence (without strong ecclesial typology)",
      "basis": [
        "Jonah 1 (ship as setting for flight and judgment)",
        "Acts 27 (Paul's voyage and shipwreck)"
      ],
      "prominence": "rare"
    }
  ],
  "has_settled_consensus": true,
  "confidence": "high",
  "notes": "The Church-as-ship (or ark) image is a long-standing, widely attested piece of Christian symbolism in patristic, liturgical, and artistic tradition; other readings exist but are secondary or context-specific rather than competing for primary status."
}
